  "dom_id": "step-q16552",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Given that $\\frac{3p+2q\\text{i}}{2-3\\text{i}}=1+2\\text{i}$, calculate $p$ and $q.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2-3\\text{i}$ to get rid of the fraction and isolate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3p+2q\\text{i}}{2-3\\text{i}} & =1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 3p+2q\\text{i} & =(1+2\\text{i})(2-3\\text{i}) \\\\ 3p+2q\\text{i} & =2-3\\text{i}+4\\text{i}-6{\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\ 3p+2q\\text{i} & =2+\\text{i}-6(-1) \\\\ 3p+2q\\text{i} & =8+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can equate the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.\n\n- Equating the real parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p & =8 \\\\ p & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the imaginary parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2q & =1 \\\\ q & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{8}{3}$ and $q=\\frac{1}{2}.$",
